"""
Attitude and airspeed reference: the sensing half of an autopilot.

This is the continuous part of a flight control system --- an attitude and
heading reference unit with a pitot source. It reads the aircraft datum frame
and the air mass and publishes pitch, bank and airspeed as ordinary signals.

Splitting it out from the controller is not cosmetic. A digital autopilot is
a signal processor: it cannot reach into a multibody frame, it reads whatever
the sensors hand it. Keeping the frame algebra on this side leaves the
controller a pure signal block, which is both how the hardware is arranged
and what makes a sampled implementation possible at all.

# Reading attitude from the frame

Column 2 of `frame_a.R` is the world vertical resolved in body axes, which
for the yaw-pitch-roll sequence of this frame convention is

    [sin(theta), cos(theta)*cos(phi), -cos(theta)*sin(phi)]

so pitch and bank come straight out of it. This is exact at any heading and
singular only with the nose straight up.

No sensor error is modelled --- no bias, no noise, no lag. Those belong here
rather than in the controller when they are wanted.
"""
component AttitudeSensor
  "Mount on the aircraft datum"
  frame_a = Frame3D() {^frame_a}
  "Density and wind, so the airspeed is true airspeed and not ground speed"
  air = AirState() {^air}
  "Pitch attitude, positive nose up"
  theta = RealOutput() {^theta}
  "Bank angle, positive right wing down"
  phi = RealOutput() {^phi}
  "Airspeed"
  V = RealOutput() {^V}

  "Airspeed regularisation, so the speed stays differentiable at rest"
  parameter v_eps::Velocity = 0.5

  "Velocity of the datum relative to the air mass, world frame"
  variable v_rel_0::Velocity[3]
relations
  # A sensor applies nothing to the frame it reads
  frame_a.f = [0, 0, 0]
  frame_a.tau = [0, 0, 0]

  v_rel_0 = der(frame_a.r_0) - [air.v_wind_x, air.v_wind_y, air.v_wind_z]
  V = sqrt(v_rel_0[1]^2 + v_rel_0[2]^2 + v_rel_0[3]^2 + v_eps^2)
  theta = atan(frame_a.R[1, 2], sqrt(frame_a.R[2, 2]^2 + frame_a.R[3, 2]^2))
  phi = atan(-frame_a.R[3, 2], frame_a.R[2, 2])
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://Multtest/AttitudeSensor.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "frame_a": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 300, "x2": 50, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "air": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 600, "x2": 50, "y2": 700}}}
    },
    "theta": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 200, "x2": 1050, "y2": 300}}}
    },
    "phi": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 450, "x2": 1050, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "V": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 700, "x2": 1050, "y2": 800}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Sampled-data attitude hold: the same two loops as `Pilot`, running on a clock.

# What is different from `Pilot`, and why it matters

`Pilot` is a continuous controller --- a reasonable model of a human, who is
not sampled. A flight computer is sampled, and three things change when you
say so rather than assuming the difference away:

* **The rate terms become backward differences of the sampled attitude**,
  `(theta_k - theta_k-1)/Ts`, not `der(theta)`. A digital controller cannot
  differentiate a continuous signal; it differences successive samples. This
  removes the continuous derivative from the loop entirely.
* **The integral becomes an explicit Euler sum**, `xi_k = xi_k-1 + Ts*e_k`,
  and the anti-windup factor has to read the *previous* output, because a
  causal discrete loop cannot use the output it is in the middle of computing.
* **The outputs are held between ticks.** The zero-order hold is explicit, so
  the roughly `Ts/2` of phase lag it costs is in the model rather than absent
  from it.

Everything else --- the gains, the `tanh` saturation, the rotation on
airspeed rather than on a clock time --- is carried over unchanged, so that
at a fast sample rate this reproduces `Pilot` and any difference between the
two is the sampling and nothing else.

# Implementation

Declared `external` and implemented in `dyad/digital_autopilot.jl`. The
clocked equations themselves are perfectly expressible --- Dyad has the shift
syntax (`x@(c - 1)`) and clock parameters (`@[input c]`) --- but this kernel
has no way to bind a concrete periodic clock to such a parameter, so the
partition comes out as an unresolved `InferredDiscrete` and compilation
fails. Writing the equations in Julia against `Clock(Ts)` is what pins the
rate. The semantics are the same synchronous ones either way.

# Sample period

`Ts` is a **structural** parameter: the clock is constructed when the
component is built, so changing the rate rebuilds the model rather than
re-running it.
"""
external component DigitalAutopilot
  "Measured pitch attitude"
  theta = RealInput() {^theta}
  "Measured bank angle"
  phi = RealInput() {^phi}
  "Measured airspeed"
  V = RealInput() {^V}
  "Elevator command, positive trailing edge down, held between ticks"
  elevator = RealOutput() {^elevator}
  "Aileron command, positive rolls right, held between ticks"
  aileron = RealOutput() {^aileron}

  "Sample period; the clock is built with the component, so this is structural"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.02
  "Pitch attitude held below the rotation speed"
  parameter theta_ground::Angle = 0.0
  "Pitch attitude held above the rotation speed"
  parameter theta_climb::Angle = 0.12
  "Airspeed at which the aircraft is rotated"
  parameter V_rotate::Velocity = 22.0
  "Width of the blend between the two commanded attitudes"
  parameter V_blend::Velocity = 1.5
  "Bank angle held throughout, positive right wing down"
  parameter phi_cmd::Angle = 0.0
  "Elevator per radian of pitch error"
  parameter k_theta::Real = 0.25
  "Elevator per radian per second of pitch rate"
  parameter kd_theta::Real = 0.06
  "Aileron per radian of bank error"
  parameter k_phi::Real = 0.35
  "Aileron per radian per second of roll rate"
  parameter kd_phi::Real = 0.05
  "Time over which a standing attitude error is trimmed away, both axes"
  parameter T_i::Real = 4.0
  "Control deflection limit, both axes"
  parameter delta_max::Angle = 0.35
relations
metadata {
  "Dyad": {
    "icons": {"default": "dyad://Multtest/DigitalAutopilot.svg"},
    "labels": [
      {"label": "$(instance)", "x": 500, "y": 1100, "rot": 0},
      {
        "label": "Ts = $(Ts)",
        "x": 500,
        "y": 1230,
        "rot": 0,
        "attrs": {"font-size": "90", "fill": "#6b7a88"}
      }
    ]
  },
  "_links": {
    "theta": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 200, "x2": 50, "y2": 300}}}
    },
    "phi": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 450, "x2": 50, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "V": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 700, "x2": 50, "y2": 800}}}
    },
    "elevator": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 300, "x2": 1050, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "aileron": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 600, "x2": 1050, "y2": 700}}}
    }
  }
}
end
